of layers and the kernel sizes to match the effective receptive field size of the shallow polar predictor, that is to say units in both models operate on the same size input region in the image. In practice, both the encoder and the decoder are composed of 4 convolutional layers, each with 64 channels, and using filter kernels of size 5 \u00d7 5 followed by half-wave rectification (ReLU). In this architecture, the encoder and the decoder are not tied together.\nThe convolutional kernels are learned by minimizing the average prediction error, measured as squared error:\nmin w Et\u2225x(t+ 1)\u2212 x\u0302(t+ 1)\u222522 We restrict the computation of this prediction error to the center of the image, for two reasons. First, moving content that enters from outside the video frame is inherently unpredictable. Second, convolution operations are generally performed with zero-padding to handle boundaries, which will artificially interfere with prediction. In practice, we trim a 17-pixel strip from each side, yielding frames of size 94\u00d7 94 pixels."
