        "heading": "3.1 PERFORMANCE",
        "text": "We summarize the prediction results in Table 1. First, observe that the predictive algorithms considered in this study can reduce the mean squared error that would be expected by simply copying the last frame by about a half. Note that when measuring the structural similarity Wang et al., a perceptual metric with values ranging from 0 to 1, these methods obtain relatively low scores, indicating that prediction on this dataset is quite challenging. Second we note that the polar predictor (PP) performs about as well as the vanilla convolutional neural network (CNN) in terms of test mean squared error. This is remarkable because the PP model has roughly 30 times fewer parameters and uses a single non-linearity, while the CNN is composed of 20 non-linear layers. This indicates that embedding a polar non-linearity in the architecture greatly contributes to performance. Finally, we also find that deepPP can outperform other methods in terms of mean squared error. It is particularly interesting to see that a deep auto-encoder with identical architecture but linear prediction mechanism does not achieve the same result. This is further indication that the polar coordinates provide an inductive bias beneficial to the prediction task.\nWhile average performance values provide a compact summary, it is also informative to examine the distribution of prediction errors on individual frames from the test set. Figure 3 shows pairwise comparison of the predictive algorithms for each frame. To make the contrast more apparent, we display performance difference on the vertical axis. Note that while the models have been optimized to reduce mean squared error, we show root mean squared error (RMSE) in order to facilitate visual inspection of the results (the concavity of the square root spreads out small differences). We see that (a) The polar predictor representation systematically outperforms causal motion compensation, especially on difficult examples. (b) The polar predictor outperforms the CNN on the bulk of easy to medium cases but this advantage is reversed for harder examples. (c) The deep polar predictor outperforms the simple polar predictor overall, indicating that the non-linearity in representation can\nhelp, but there are many exceptions. (d) The deep polar predictor clearly outperforms the deep linear predictor, revealing the strong benefit of using a polar extrapolation mechanism over a linear one."
